 # Fast tri-cubic interpolation
 
-Also called: Tri-cubic interpolation, GPU-accelerated tri-cubic interpolation, Fast tri-cubic reconstruction
+Also called: Tri-cubic interpolation
 
 ## Overview
-Fast tri-cubic interpolation is a GPU-based higher-order reconstruction scheme that evaluates a tri-cubic B-spline filter by reducing the number of texture fetches required for the convolution sum, leveraging GPU linear filtering capabilities [S1]. It solves the performance bottleneck of standard cubic interpolation, which requires 64 texel fetches for a 3D tri-cubic filter, by using separable filter kernels stored in 1D textures and pre-computed weight/offset functions to achieve the same result with only eight tri-linear texture fetches [S1]. It sets itself apart from [Bilinear Interpolation](858-bilinear-interpolation.md) and [Multi-Texture Trilinear Estimation](1164-multi-texture-trilinear-estimation.md) by providing C1 continuous reconstruction with higher frequency accuracy, while avoiding the high memory bandwidth cost of fetching all 64 neighbors [S1]. It differs from [Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling](826-frequency-domain-volume-rendering-with-sinc-resampling.md) by operating in the spatial domain using fixed B-spline kernels rather than windowed sinc functions in the frequency domain [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Fast tri-cubic interpolation is a GPU-based higher-order reconstruction scheme that evaluates a tri-cubic B-spline filter by reducing the number of texture fetches required for the convolution sum, leveraging GPU linear filtering capabilities [S1]. It solves the performance bottleneck of standard cubic interpolation, which requires 64 texel fetches for a 3D tri-cubic filter, by using separable filter kernels stored in 1D textures and pre-computed weight/offset functions to achieve the same result with only eight tri-linear texture fetches [S1]. It differs from [Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling](826-frequency-domain-volume-rendering-with-sinc-resampling.md) by operating in the spatial domain using fixed B-spline kernels rather than windowed sinc functions in the frequency domain [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
